Yes. Once you separate presentation, embodiment, rules, and agency, the design space gets much larger.

You can keep a character’s exact capability envelope—same speed, powers, cooldowns, weaknesses, inventory limits, hitbox logic, stamina model, even quirks—while swapping the body, world, texture set, animation language, faction, or narrative role around it. That lets you test what is actually essential to the mechanic versus what merely came bundled with its original fiction.

The useful decomposition becomes something like:

Identity skin → appearance, voice, species, faction, animation, textures
Embodiment → size, mass, locomotion, collision, reach, sensory range
Capability set → powers, weapons, traversal, limitations, cooldowns
Decision policy → how the entity chooses actions
Memory → what it remembers about you/world/events
Goals → persistent objectives and preferences
Social model → alliances, grudges, reputation, trust
World rules → physics, economy, geography, resources
Interface layer → how all of those systems communicate

Then you can deliberately hold some layers fixed while mutating others.

For example, take a classic boss with its original powers and limitations, drop it physically unchanged into a modern open-world ecosystem, and replace the old scripted AI with an agent that has memory, planning, self-preservation, and social awareness.

Now the question stops being:

“Can I beat the boss?”

and becomes:

“What does this creature do when nobody is fighting it?”

Maybe it hunts. Maybe nearby NPCs learn its migration pattern. Merchants reroute caravans. Hunters form parties. The creature learns that the player uses fire attacks and begins avoiding dry terrain. Another agent discovers that and sells the information. The boss may even decide you are the recurring environmental hazard.

That is where agentic NPCs become transformative.

Traditional NPC:

state → trigger → predefined response

Agentic NPC:

perception → memory → internal model → motives → planning → action → consequence → learning

And if you preserve original powers, you can perform almost scientific transplantation experiments.

Imagine putting:

* a Pokémon with its canonical moves and weaknesses into Skyrim;
* a Left 4 Dead-style AI director into Pokémon;
* a Metal Gear guard perception model into a medieval RPG;
* Civilization-style diplomacy into factions made of individual NPC agents;
* Mario movement physics into an otherwise realistic world;
* a Souls boss into a world where NPCs can independently organize to fight it;
* Minecraft crafting rules inside a survival game whose NPCs can also discover recipes.

Then observe the emergent delta.

That last piece matters: don’t immediately rebalance everything. Initially preserve the foreign system as faithfully as possible. Let incompatibilities reveal themselves.

System A + Environment B → emergent behavior C

Some failures are bugs. Others reveal entirely new mechanics.

A ten-foot jump that was mundane in its original game might become supernatural somewhere else. A weak creature may become devastating because the new world’s NPCs don’t understand its attack pattern. A seemingly useless power may become economically dominant because it interacts with another game’s resource system.

You can even automate this into a mechanic collider:

select entity/system
→ freeze desired invariants
→ select host world
→ translate interfaces
→ spawn agentic inhabitants
→ simulate
→ measure deltas
→ identify emergent strategies
→ retain interesting mutations
→ recombine again

And texture/character replacement gives you another diagnostic instrument. Reskin the same mechanics repeatedly and see how human perception changes.

A fireball fired by a wizard feels expected.

Give the exact same projectile physics, damage curve, cooldown, and targeting mechanism to a biotech flower that launches glowing seeds and suddenly players may interpret the identical mechanic as defensive biology rather than magic.

So even presentation itself becomes an experimental variable.

That gives us four distinct operations:

Reskin — same rules, new appearance.
Rebody — same capabilities, different physical embodiment.
Transplant — same system, different game/world.
Agentify — same entity, but give it memory, goals, planning, and adaptation.

Then combine them.

And once you start preserving the provenance—where each mechanic came from, which properties were retained, which interfaces were translated, and what emerged after transplantation—you effectively get a phylogenetic tree of game mechanics.

Not just a game engine anymore.

A laboratory for artificial worlds.